\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around seven findings (F1--F7) that establish a causal chain from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$, \S\ref{sec:metrics}).

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 1: Main results summary
%% ============================================================

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{All configurations and outcomes. NVE reports peak $\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\S\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded; $\dagger$\,marks a false positive (non-CIVO collapse with elevated NVE).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Exp} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{dt\_emb} & \textbf{Sub} & \textbf{H} & \textbf{Eval@final} & \textbf{NVE} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
001 & Baseline ($\dt{=}1$) & --- & --- & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\times$ & Healthy \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
006 & Fixed $\dt{=}2$ & trn & ON & 15 & 132.9 @65K & 4.83$\times$ & Collapsed \\
007 & Fixed $\dt{=}4$ & trn & ON & 15 & 152.5 @55K & --- & Collapsed \\
005 & Learned gate & adaptive & OFF & 15 & 98.8 @145K & --- & Collapsed \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{gray!15}
008d & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\times$ & Full collapse \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
009b & Factorial (s2) & trn & ON & 8 & 267.9 @105K & 1.62$\times$ & Transient CIVO \\
008f & No dt\_emb (s1) & --- & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.97$\times$ & Healthy \\
008a & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.93$\times$ & Healthy \\
015b & Frozen emb & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 204.2 @105K & 0.47$\times$ & Grad.\ starvation \\
\midrule
018 s1 & Full-SGS & trn$^\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & \sgseval{} @105K & \sgsnve{}$\times$$^*$ & No collapse \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
018 s2 & Full-SGS & trn$^\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & 40.8 @105K & --- & Collapsed \\
008e\_v3 s1 & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 869.9 @105K & 0.80$\times$ & Healthy \\
008e\_v3 s2$^\dagger$ & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 137.0 @105K & 3.78$\times$ & Non-CIVO collapse \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\textit{Cross-environment validation (Cheetah Run)}} \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
010b & Cheetah Run & trn & ON & 8 & 51.7 @105K & 14.59$\times$ & Collapsed \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize
trn = trained ($d{=}32$, subsample ON);
trn$^\ddagger$ = full-pathway SGS (all non-KL gradients detached);
exp$^a$ = trained, subsample OFF;
frz$^b$ = frozen ($d{=}32$, \texttt{requires\_grad=False});
--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\
$^*$ Seed 1 only: peak NVE with transient exceedances (1.61$\times$ at 35K, 1.66$\times$ at 95K); self-correcting, no sustained escalation. Seed 2 collapsed.\par}
\end{table*}

%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the causal trigger. Comparing exp\_008d (trained, subsample ON) against exp\_008f (no embedding, subsample ON): 008d reaches $\nve = 4.12\times$ and collapses (eval 82.8), while 008f maintains $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 7/8 seeds (AUROC${=}0.933$; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, $n{=}2$) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed); no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes: seed~1 (008d) undergoes full collapse ($\nve = 4.12\times$, eval 82.8), while seed~2 (009b) self-corrects after transient $\nve = 1.62\times$ (eval 267.9). Both exceed $\nve \geq 1.5\times$, confirming deterministic triggering with seed-dependent outcomes (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly necessary} for CIVO. Neither factor alone is sufficient: unexposed trained embedding (008a) maintains NVE $\leq 0.93\times$, frozen embedding (015b) never exceeds $0.47\times$, and removed embedding (008f) shows NVE $\leq 0.97\times$. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$).

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions and cross-environment
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions and Multiple Pathways}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination does not prevent all collapse.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Seed~1 is healthy (eval 869.9, $\nve = 0.80\times$); seed~2 collapses (eval $715 \rightarrow 137$, $\nve = 3.78\times$)---but via a qualitatively distinct mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). Classic CIVO (008d, Pathway~A) shows value explosion followed by evaluation decline with decreasing entropy; 008e\_v3\_s2 (Pathway~B) shows evaluation crash with \emph{increasing} entropy ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$) while values remain stable. This produces the sole false positive in the NVE classification.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO)---value explosion, entropy decreases. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)---evaluation crashes, entropy increases.}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Exp\_018 applies Selective Gradient Stopping (SGS) by detaching all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$, blocking the observation decoder pathway identified in F2 as the primary CIVO conduit. Figure~\ref{fig:civo_decomposition} decomposes evaluation returns across conditions. Trained $\dt$-embedding alone produces $-73\%$ peak and $-86\%$ final eval degradation relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 avoids collapse (eval \sgseval{}, $-51\%$ final relative to baseline) while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8, $-96\%$), yielding a 50\% prevention rate. SGS reduces but does not eliminate CIVO risk: the gradient pathway it blocks is necessary but not sufficient for reliable prevention. Reward-only SGS (016e) fails entirely (NVE $10.42\times$). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah Run (010b) confirms CIVO generalization: evaluation peaks at 321.9 before collapsing to 22.7 ($-93\%$); peak NVE $14.59\times$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_civo_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{CIVO decomposition: peak and final (105K step) evaluation returns on Walker Walk (DMC) across four conditions. \textbf{Baseline} (001): vanilla DreamerV3, no temporal embedding. \textbf{Trained dt-emb} (008d, 009b): DreamerV3 with a learned $d{=}32$ temporal embedding exposed to multi-rate data, two seeds. \textbf{Full-SGS} (s1, s2): trained embedding with Selective Gradient Stopping that detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$, two seeds. Trained $\dt$-embedding degrades peak eval by $73\%$ and final eval by $86\%$ relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 partially recovers ($-51\%$ final, no collapse) but seed~2 collapses ($-96\%$ final, marked ``collapsed''), indicating that SGS reduces but does not reliably prevent CIVO (prevention rate $= 50\%$). All percentages relative to Baseline 001.}
\label{fig:civo_decomposition}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 2: RSSM Fidelity
%% ============================================================

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{RSSM fidelity at $\dt{=}1$ vs.\ $\dt{=}2$. Trained embedding (008d) produces $2.78\times$ reward gap; removing it (008f) eliminates the gap but degrades absolute prediction.}
\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{obs\_MSE}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{reward\_MAE}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7}
\textbf{Config} & $\dt{=}1$ & $\dt{=}2$ & Gap & $\dt{=}1$ & $\dt{=}2$ & Gap \\
\midrule
Baseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\times$ \\
008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & 2.78$\times$ \\
008f (no emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The trained embedding in 008d produces a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), providing the systematic bias that the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates.

